|~ The module of the noun `::qlang`, the language itself: its first
   page, the first screen of a reader, and its members,
   the concepts of the language, each a page read through the noun,
   `::qlang | doc :pipeline`, in the order they rest on each other:
   values, the pipeline, verbs, errors, code, the hypertext and the
   hosts. The first screen names them all. ~|

::qlang
  |~~ qlang is a pipeline language over values. A query is a subject and its steps: each step is a command on the value the step before it answered, and answers a new value; nothing changes in place, and the answer is the last value. Everything the language handles is a value with a literal that prints and reads back as itself: data, code, prose and failures alike. So the language describes itself in its own values: every name has a page, the pages grow from ::qlang, and a query reads them like any data.

      Every `~(…)` on this page is a law: a query that runs and answers true. A form copied from a law is a form that works.

      What a reader can rely on, the invariants every rule below follows from:
      - A query is a function of its input: the same subject answers the same value, nothing changes in place, and nothing is read or written outside it but by the verbs of the host.
      - The value in the pipe is the one implicit argument: every command takes it as its subject, and every modifier is computed against it.
      - Every nested piece, an element of a literal, a group, the body of `*`, a modifier, starts from the value and the names around it and gives back only its value.
      - Nothing is converted behind the reader: no truthiness, no number read out of a string, no default for a missing part; a value that does not fit makes its step fail, under a tag naming that step.
      - One order ranks every value, so anything sorts, compares and dedupes.
      - A failure is a value: it rides past `|` and `*` until `!|` opens it.
      - Every value prints as a literal that reads back as itself: what an answer shows is the value.
      - What exists is what the language lists: a verb missing from the list of a kind does not exist for it, and a page tells the truth about its name because its laws run.

      1. The combinators. Steps are joined by combinators, each set off by spaces on both sides; they bind alike, from left to right.
      `a | b` runs b on the value a answered.
      `a * b` runs b on each element of a: a vector or a set answers the vector of the results, a map answers a map with the same keys. Several steps after `*` stand in a group, and a pipe written before `*` or `!|` stands for it.
      `a !| b` runs b only when a answered a failure; any other value passes it untouched.
      `(…)` is a group: its steps run on the value in the pipe and answer their result.
      At the top level a line is a step, joined to the next line by `|`.
      ~([1 2 3] * (mul 2 | add 1) | eq [3 5 7])
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} * (mul 10) | eq {:a 10 :b 20})
      ~([1 "x" 3] * (mul 2 !| 0) | eq [2 0 6])
      ~(5 | (add 1 | mul 2) | eq 12)

      2. The literals. A literal is a step: it replaces the value in the pipe, and every word inside it is computed against that value.
      42, -1.5 and 1e3 are numbers, finite doubles. "text" is a string. :name and :"two words" are keywords, names that stand for themselves. null, true and false. [1 2 3] is a vector, [1, 2, 3] the same vector. {:k 1} is a map, and {"k": 1} the same map: every key of a map is a keyword. #[3 1 2] is a set. `~(add 1)` is code, a quote. |~~ text ~~| is prose, a doc. !{:reason :x} is a failure. ::Box is a tag name, a value of its own. ::Box{:a 1} is a value under the tag ::Box.
      ~(5 | [1 2] | eq [1 2])
      ~({"a": 1, "b": [true, null]} | eq {:a 1 :b [true null]})
      ~([7 8] | ::Box | eq ::Box)
      A literal is how data is reshaped: write the shape the answer takes, with its parts read from the value.
      ~({:first "Ann" :age 30} | {:who /first :next (/age | add 1)} | eq {:who "Ann" :next 31})
      ~([{:id 1 :tags [:a :b]}] * {:id /id :n (/tags | count)} | eq [{:id 1 :n 2}])

      3. Reading parts. /k reads a key of a map, /0 an element of a vector, /-1 counts from the end, /a/b walks a path, / alone is the whole value. A part that is not there is a failure; `at` reads softly and answers null, and `coalesce` puts a fallback in its place, `at :k | coalesce 0`.
      ~({:a {:b [10 20]}} | /a/b/-1 | eq 20)
      ~({"first name": "Ann"} | /"first name" | eq "Ann")
      ~({:a 1} | /b !| type | eq ::ProjectionKeyNotInMapError)
      ~({:a 1} | at :b | eq null)
      ~({:a 1} | at :b | coalesce 0 | eq 0)

      4. Commands. A command is a name and its modifiers, one word each, separated by spaces: `add 1`, `split ","`, `filter ~(gt 1)`. The subject comes through the pipe, and every modifier is computed against the subject, so a command can read two fields of it. A command with modifiers inside a literal or a modifier stands in parentheses.
      ~({:price 4 :qty 3} | mul /price /qty | eq 12)
      ~({:a 1} | [(/a | add 1) /a] | eq [2 1])
      A modifier that is code, which a verb runs later on each element, is a quote: `~(…)`, or `~name` for one word. A value computed at the call is refused there.
      ~([{:age 3} {:age 20}] | filter ~(/age | gte 18) | count | eq 1)
      ~([{:n 2} {:n 1}] | sort ~/n | first | eq {:n 1})
      ~([1 2 3] | filter (gt 1) !| type | eq ::FilterPredicateNotQuoteError)

      5. Kinds and verbs. Every value has a kind, which `type` answers as a tag: ::number ::string ::keyword ::boolean ::null ::vec ::map ::set, and ::quote ::doc ::tag and others. A verb lives on the kinds it serves, and a name finds its verb through the kind of its subject; what a kind answers is listed by `::string | spec | /verbs`, and a verb outside its list does not exist for it. The verbs most queries use:
      numbers: add sub mul div round, and gt gte lt lte to compare.
      strings: split lines contains startsWith endsWith lower upper append prepend parseJson keyword, and count take drop by characters; strings join from a vector by `join ","`.
      containers: count first last at take drop filter every any sort reverse distinct flat sum min max reduce groupBy indexBy has contains, and for maps keys vals entries; union minus inter for sets and maps. The greatest by a key is `sort ~(key) | last`, `sort ~(/n | desc) ~(/name)` ranks by n descending and then by name, and `flat` splices one level of nesting.
      every value: type eq ne print json if cond coalesce apply tag payload.
      booleans: and or not.
      ~([42 "s" :k [1] {:k 1} #[1] true null] * type | eq [::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set ::boolean ::null])
      ~("a,b,c" | split "," | join "-" | eq "a-b-c")
      ~("db_host" | [count (drop 3) (take 2)] | eq [7 "host" "db"])
      ~(["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy * count | eq {:b 2 :a 1})
      ~({:auth {:port 3000}} | entries * (union /1 {:name (/0 | keyword)}) | eq [{:port 3000 :name "auth"}])
      ~({:a 1} | entries | tag ::map | eq {:a 1})
      ~(3.14159 | round 2 | ne 3.14159)
      ~([3 1 2] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 6)

      6. What differs from the languages a reader knows.
      One order ranks every value: null, then booleans, numbers, strings, keywords, tag names, vectors, sets, maps; so anything sorts. A set is the vector in that order without duplicates: `distinct` and `keys` answer a set, `take`, `drop` and `filter` keep a set, and `sort` and `reverse` answer a vector.
      ~(#[:z :x :y] | first | eq :x)
      ~([2 3 2 1] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])
      ~({:y 1 :x 2} | keys | eq #[:x :y])
      ~(#[:z :x :y] | reverse | eq [:z :y :x])
      A map's elements are its values: `count`, `sum`, `first`, `filter`, `sort` and `*` read the values, and the keys travel with them.
      ~({:a 1 :b 5} | filter ~(gt 2) | eq {:b 5})
      A condition is a boolean: `if`, `filter`, `every`, `any` and `cond` refuse any other value, so nothing is truthy.
      ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("pos") ~("neg") | eq "pos")
      ~(5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no") !| type | eq ::IfConditionNotBooleanError)
      A string and a keyword turn into each other by `keyword`; text and code turn into each other by `parse`; `print` answers the text a value reads back from, a string.
      ~("draft" | keyword | eq :draft)
      ~(:draft | keyword | eq "draft")
      ~(7 | print | eq "7")
      A name, `:n v`, holds v for the steps after it, and the value in the pipe goes on unchanged; `:n /` names the value in the pipe. A name is declared once in a scope, and the names a group declares close with it.
      ~(5 | :n / | [n (n | add 1)] | eq [5 6])
      ~(:k 1 | :k 2 !| type | eq ::BindNameDeclaredTwiceError)

      7. Failures. A step that fails answers a failure, a value: `|` and `*` pass it by, and `!|` opens it to its facts. The tag of a failure names the site that refused it: the verb, the place, and what the place takes, so ::AddLeftNotNumberError is `add` refusing its left operand, its subject, for not being a number, and ::FilterPredicateNotQuoteError is `filter` refusing a predicate that is no quote. `!| explain` shows the page of the site.
      ~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::AddLeftNotNumberError)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| /actualType | eq ::string)
      ~([1 2] | add 1 !| type | eq ::AddLeftNotNumberError)
      ~(!{:reason :late} !| /reason | eq :late)
      A failure is built from a map by `raise`, or written as a literal with the tag of its own kind before the bang; `!|` opens it to that map, its tag stacked over the kind ::error. Whether a value is a failure is `false !| true`, and `/trail` holds the path it took, the steps it skipped last.
      ~({:reason :late} | raise !| [type /reason] | eq [::error :late])
      ~(::Late!{:by 3} !| [type /by] | eq [::Late 3])
      ~([!{:k 1} 5] * (false !| true) | eq [true false])
      ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| /trail/-1/skipped | eq ~(mul 2))

      8. Code as a value. A quote holds steps unrun: it is the vector of its steps, `apply` runs it on a subject, and `parse` turns text into a quote and a quote into its text. A verb of your own is a name over `::verb~(…)`, whose leading declarations are its slots; a kind of your own is `::Name {:impl ~(…)}`, whose quote checks or builds every value put under it.
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 2)
      ~(5 | apply ~(add 1 | mul 2) | eq 12)
      ~("add 1" | parse | eq ~(add 1))
      ~(:inc ::verb~(:by ::number | add by) | 5 | inc 2 | eq 7)
      A slot declared by a kind must be filled; by a literal it is filled with that literal when the call leaves it out; by a set of kinds with ::null among them it may be left out and reads null; `* :name kind` gathers the modifiers that remain; `:returns kind` checks the answer. A verb runs when its name is mentioned, and stands where code is asked by `~name`; a quote held in a name runs by `apply`.
      ~(:f ::verb~(:by 10 | add by) | 1 | f | eq 11)
      ~(:f ::verb~(* :xs ::number | xs | sum) | null | f 1 2 3 | eq 6)
      ~(:pos ::verb~(gt 0) | [1 -2 3] | filter ~pos | eq [1 3])
      ~(:q ~(add 1) | 5 | apply q | eq 6)
      A step of a quote is a record of what it computes, a command under ::call with its `:name`:
      ~(~(add 1) | first | [type /name] | eq [::call :add])
      ~(::Small {:impl ~(if (lt 10) ~(/) ~(!{:reason :big}))} | ::Small(12) !| /reason | eq :big)
      Prose is a value too: |~~ text ~~| is a doc, whose `content` is its text, `segments` its prose and quotes, and `laws` the quotes that run. A comment, written |~ like this ~| or from |~| to the end of its line, is whitespace.
      ~(|~~ a ~(eq 1) b ~~| | [(segments | count) (laws | count)] | eq [3 1])

      9. Working a task. Look at the input before shaping it: `type`, `keys`, `/0`, `count`. Write the shape of the answer as a literal, and build the query one step at a time, running each. When a step fails, its tag names the verb and the place; `!| explain` reads the page of that site, and `::vec/sort | doc` the page of a verb, with laws to copy.
      ~([{:id 1 :tags ["a"]}] | /0 | keys | eq #[:id :tags])

      10. Asking. `x | doc` answers the page of a name or of the kind of a value: its text is `content`, its laws `laws`, its links `links`. ::snippet~(::qlang | manifest) lists the kinds, ::snippet~(::vec | spec | /verbs) the verbs of a kind by address, `::vec/sort | doc` the page of one verb, `::any/sort | doc` the page every `sort` shares, and `::vec/sort | runLaws` runs its laws. `x !| explain` shows the page of what refused. An answer prints whole; `x | elide 4000` bounds it, each part left out an ::elision whose `:read` continues the query to it.
      ~(::qlang | doc | links * open | count | gt 5)

      11. The chapters, in the order they rest on each other, each page naming its neighbours and the kinds that are it:
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :values) values, kinds and tags;
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :pipeline) the steps of a query;
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :names) names and their records;
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :verbs) the verbs that live on kinds;
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :errors) the failure as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :code) code as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :hypertext) the pages and how to move through them;
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :hosts) the tool the language runs in and the budget of an answer.
  ~~|

:values
  |~~ Values. Everything qlang handles is a value, data, code, prose and
      failures alike, so everything the language says about itself is a
      value the pipeline reads. A value never changes, and each has a
      kind, the name of which is a tag: it tells how to read the value it
      stands on, and the tag taken as a value, `::Box`, is the kind
      itself, which a query asks for its page and its verbs. Rests on
      nothing; the pipeline, the verbs and the hypertext rest on it.
      ::snippet~(::qlang | manifest) lists the kinds. ::link~(::tag | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :pipeline)

      A literal implies its kind:
      ~([42 "s" :k [1] {:k 1} #[1] true null] * type | eq [::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set ::boolean ::null])
      A tag written before a payload puts it under the tag, whatever
      brackets the payload takes, `::T{…}`, `::T[…]`, `::T(…)` or `::T"…"`:
      ~([::Box{:a 1} ::Box[1 2] ::Box(1 | add 2) ::Box"s"] * payload | eq [{:a 1} [1 2] 3 "s"])
      `tag` puts the value in the pipe under a tag, tags stack and read
      from the outside in, and `payload` takes one off:
      ~([7 8] | tag ::Inner | tag ::Outer | [type (payload | type) (payload | payload)] | eq [::Outer ::Inner [7 8]])
      ~(::Box::Crate{:a 1} | type | eq ::Box)
      A step answers a new value and the old one stays as it was:
      ~([1 2] | :v / | append 3 | [v /] | eq [[1 2] [1 2 3]])
      Every value prints as a literal that reads back as itself:
      ~({:a [1 #[2]] :t ::Box[3]} | :v / | print | parse | :q / | apply q | eq v)
  ~~|
  #[::tag ::null ::boolean ::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set]

:pipeline
  |~~ The pipeline. A query is a subject and its steps, each a command
      on the value the step before it answered, and the answer is the
      last value; the shape of an answer is written as the literal it
      takes, so a query names what it reads. Rests on values; the verbs
      are its commands, and code holds its steps as a value.
      ::link~(::each | doc) ::link~(::proj | doc)
      ::link~(::group | doc) ::link~(::qlang | doc :names)

      `|` runs the next step on the answer of the one before:
      ~(5 | add 1 | mul 2 | eq 12)
      A literal is a step that replaces the value in the pipe, a tag name
      among them, so a value goes under a tag by `tag`:
      ~(5 | [1 2] | eq [1 2])
      ~([7 8] | ::Box | eq ::Box)
      `*` stands in the place of `|` and runs its step on each element of
      a vector or each value of a map, a literal among its steps:
      ~([{:id 1 :tags [1 2]}] * {:id /id :n (/tags | count)} | eq [{:id 1 :n 2}])
      A pipe written before `*` or `!|` stands for the combinator after it:
      ~([1 2] | * (add 1) | eq [2 3])
      `/k`, `/0`, `/-1`, `/a/b` read a part, and a miss is an error:
      ~({:a {:b 1}} | /a/b | eq 1)
      ~({:a 1} | /b !| type | eq ::ProjectionKeyNotInMapError)
      A name holds the value in the pipe for the steps after it, and
      steps in parentheses run on the value in the pipe and answer their
      result:
      ~(5 | :n / | [n (n | add 1)] | eq [5 6])
      At the top level a line is a step, joined to the next by `|` unless
      one of them carries its combinator, and inside brackets a line
      break is a space; a comment, `|~ … ~|`, nesting, or `|~|` to the
      end of its line, is whitespace wherever a space stands:
      ~("[3 1 2]\n|~| the order\nsort\n| first" | parse | apply / | eq 1)
      ~("|~ outer |~ inner ~| ~| [1 2] |~ a note ~| | count" | parse | apply / | eq 2)
      `|`, `*` and `!|` bind alike, from left to right. Each part of a
      literal, a group and the body of `*` is a fork: it starts from the
      value and the names outside it, and only its answer leaves it, so
      the parts of one literal see nothing of each other:
      ~(:n 10 | [1 2] * (add n) | eq [11 12])
      ~([(:a 1 | a) (a)] | /1 !| type | eq ::UnresolvedIdentifierError)
  ~~|
  #[::each ::proj ::group ::bind ::call ::construct]

:names
  |~~ Names. A name holds a value for the steps after it, the one way a
      query remembers: `:n v` declares it in the scope where it stands,
      and a verb, a kind and a module's declarations are names alike. A
      name is a record, `::binding`, with its page, its source, its value
      and the module it came from, so where a thing was declared is read
      as data. The scope of a step is what the steps before it wrote; a
      step inside `(…)` or `*` writes a scope that closes with it. Rests
      on values and the pipeline; the verbs are names that run.
      ::link~(::binding | doc) ::link~(::bind | doc)
      ::link~(::any/use | doc) ::link~(::qlang | doc :verbs)

      `env` lists the names a query and a session wrote:
      ~(:limit 10 | env | keys | eq #[:limit])
      A name reads its record by `binding`:
      ~(:x 1 | :x | binding | /value | eq 1)
      ~(::vec/filter | binding | /module | eq :qlang/vec)
      A name declared inside a group closes with it:
      ~(1 | (:inner 2 | inner) | env | has :inner | not)
      `use` brings the declarations of a map or a module into the names
      of a reader:
      ~({:x 1} | use | x | add 2 | eq 3)
      A name is declared once in a scope and again in a scope inside it,
      which shadows it for the steps after, a verb of the core staying
      one address away:
      ~(:k 1 | :k 2 !| type | eq ::BindNameDeclaredTwiceError)
      ~(:k 1 | (:k 2 | k) | eq 2)
      ~(:count 5 | [1 2 3] | vec/count | eq 3)
      A name begins with a letter of any script, `_` or `@`; `@` marks a
      name whose call reaches outside the language, and a verb whose body
      calls one carries it too, while a name over the value a call
      answered is plain:
      ~(:@io ::verb~(1) | :fine (@io) | fine | eq 1)
      ~(:quiet ::verb~(@out) !| type | eq ::EffectLaunderingAtBindStepParseError)
      The docs in the slot of a declaration, between its name and its
      body, are its page, one doc each:
      ~(:x |~~ One. ~~| |~~ Two. ~~| 1 | env | /x | /docs | count | eq 2)
      A verb resolves its names in the scope of its declaration, itself
      among them, so it recurses by name, and the frames of a runaway
      meet a budget:
      ~(:walk ::verb~({:n /n :kids (/kids * walk)}) | {:n 1 :kids [{:n 2 :kids []}]} | walk | /kids/0/n | eq 2)
      ~(:inf ::verb~(add 1 | inf) | 0 | inf !| type | eq ::EvaluationDepthExceededError)
  ~~|
  #[::binding]

:verbs
  |~~ Verbs. A verb lives on a kind, the one whose values it serves, and
      has an address, `::vec/sort`; so what can be done with a value is
      asked of its kind, and one word serves many kinds. The verbs of a
      kind and those of every value are the whole of them: a verb in
      neither list does not exist. Rests on values and the pipeline;
      errors name the verb that refused.
      ::snippet~(::vec | spec | /verbs) lists the verbs of a kind and
      ::snippet~(::qlang/any | spec | /verbs) those of every value.
      ::link~(::verb | doc) ::link~(::qlang | doc :errors)

      A name finds its verb through the kind of its subject: its tags
      from the outside in, then the shape beneath them, then the verbs
      every value answers, on `::qlang/any`; a verb of vectors reaches a
      vector under a tag and keeps the tag:
      ~([3 1 2] | sort | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(::Box[3 1 2] | sort | eq ::Box[1 2 3])
      A verb declares its slots and the kinds it takes, which `spec`
      answers before a call:
      ~(::vec/sort | spec | type | eq ::spec)
      A reader's verb is a name over `::verb` and a quote whose head
      declares its slots, and it takes its arguments after its name:
      ~(:inc ::verb~(:by ::number | add by) | 5 | inc 2 | eq 7)
      A modifier is evaluated at the call against the subject, and when
      the call writes every place of the verb, the subject among them,
      the value in the pipe is only where they are read; a slot that runs
      code later takes a quote, `~name` or `~/path` the quote of one step, and a
      condition is a boolean:
      ~({:price 100 :qty 3} | mul /price /qty | eq 300)
      ~([{:age 3} {:age 20}] | filter ~(/age | gt 18) | count | eq 1)
      ~([{:age 3}] | sort ~/age | first | /age | eq 3)
      ~([1 2] | filter ~(1) !| type | eq ::FilterConditionNotBooleanError)
      A kind is declared by the step `::T body`, the tag, a space and a
      map whose `:impl` is a quote, the constructor that checks or builds
      each value put under it and answers an error for one it refuses:
      ~(::Small {:impl ~(if (lt 10) ~(/) ~(!{:reason :tooBig}))} | [(::Small(3) | type) (::Small(12) !| /reason)] | eq [::Small :tooBig])
      Written close, `::T{…}` is a value under the tag and declares
      nothing, so the tag checks nothing:
      ~(::Small{:impl ~(!{:reason :no})} | ::Small(12) | payload | eq 12)
  ~~|
  #[::verb ::spec ::qlang/any]

:errors
  |~~ Errors. A failure does not throw: a step that fails answers an
      error, a value that carries the facts of the failure, the path it
      took and the tag of the site that refused, which names the page of
      its procedure. A reader learning a tool fails often, and the
      failure is where what it needs is cheapest to give. Rests on
      values, the pipeline and verbs; the hypertext holds the pages the
      sites name.
      ::link~(::error | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::explanation | doc)

      A failed step answers an error the tag of its site names:
      ~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::AddLeftNotNumberError)
      `|` passes an error by and `*` answers the first an element answers:
      ~([1 "x" 3] * (mul 2) !| type | eq ::MulLeftNotNumberError)
      `a !| b` runs `b` on an error alone, opened to its facts, and
      recovers each failure where it is read:
      ~([1 "x"] * (mul 2 !| 0) | eq [2 0])
      The facts name what refused, and the first stop of the trail holds
      the value the step refused:
      ~("a" | mul 2 !| [/actualType /trail/0/subject] | eq [::string "a"])
      `explain` shows the page of the site above the error:
      ~("x" | add 1 !| explain | type | eq ::explanation)
      The trail holds a stop for each level the error left, and the last
      stop the steps it skipped, a quote ready to replay:
      ~(:f ::verb~(:n ::number | add n | mul n) | "x" | f 2 | sub 3 !| /trail * /step | eq [~(add n) ~(f 2)])
      ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| /trail/-1/skipped | parse | eq "mul 2")
      A map raised again after `!|` resumes the trail it holds, and one
      without `:trail` starts it over:
      ~(!{:kind :oops} | count !| raise | add 1 !| /trail * /step | eq [~(!{:kind :oops})])
      ~(!{:kind :oops} | count !| minus #[:trail] | raise | add 1 !| /trail * /step | eq [~(raise)])
      Whether a value is an error is `false !| true`:
      ~([!{:kind :oops} 42] * (false !| true) | eq [true false])
  ~~|
  #[::error ::fail ::explanation]

:code
  |~~ Code. A quote, `~(…)`, holds steps unrun as a value: a verb takes
      one where it needs code, `filter`, `sort`, `if`, `apply` runs one on
      a subject, and a quote is taken apart and built like any vector of
      steps. Because code is a value, a page holds laws that run and
      links that open, and the trail of an error holds the steps it
      skipped, ready to replay. Rests on values and the pipeline; the
      hypertext is written in it.
      ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::qlang | doc :hypertext)

      A quote held as a value runs by `apply`:
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | :q / | 5 | apply q | eq 12)
      A quote is the vector of its steps:
      ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 2)
  ~~|
  #[::quote]

:hypertext
  |~~ The hypertext. The names are a space of addresses, `::` beginning
      an absolute one, `::vec/sort`, and a verb passes from an address
      into one of its projections, as a path reaches a file: `doc` into
      its page, `spec` into its declaration, `source` into the code that
      declared it, and `runLaws` runs the laws of its page. An address holds no one
      entity but a set of values, and each passage reads one of them; the
      address, the verb and what it reaches are all values, so the
      pipeline goes on from a projection as from any value, and a tag
      you declare with verbs on it adds projections of its own.
      A keyword after `doc` is an anchor within the page of its subject,
      `::qlang | doc :pipeline`, a vector of anchors a path, a set of
      anchors several pages at once. A page is a doc, prose that holds
      code: a quote with no tag is a law, which `runLaws` holds to
      answer true, a quote under `::link` is a link to another page, and
      a quote under `::snippet` a query a reader runs to see what it
      shows; `open` runs either. So a page leads on to the pages that go deeper,
      and the pipeline moves through them as through any data:
      `manifest` lists the kinds, `spec | /verbs` the verbs of a kind,
      `links * open` opens every page a page names in one query, and a
      filter over pages by `content | contains` searches them. Nothing
      is read that was not asked for, and a query names the shape of
      what it reads. Rests on every concept before it.
      ::link~(::doc | doc) ::link~(::link | doc) ::link~(::snippet | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :hosts)

      The links of a page open in one query:
      ~(::qlang | doc | links * open | count | gt 0)
      One address passes into its page, its declaration and its source:
      ~(::vec/take | [(doc | type) (spec | type) (source | type)] | eq [::doc ::spec ::quote])
      A filter over pages searches them:
      ~(::vec | spec | /verbs | filter ~(doc | content | contains "after the first") | eq #[::vec/drop])
  ~~|
  #[::doc ::link ::snippet]

:hosts
  |~~ Hosts. The language runs inside a host, a tool that embeds it and
      brings verbs of its own; the command line is one. A value is whole
      in the language, and the host prints an answer whole unless a
      reader asks for a budget, by `elide` in the query or by the host's
      flag, the command line's `--budget=N`: what a budget left out is
      an `::elision` marker, whose `:read` continues the query to the
      part. The command line reads JSON or text from stdin and writes
      its answer back in the same form. Rests on values and the hypertext.
      ::link~(::elision | doc) ::link~(::any/elide | doc)

      A value whose print fits its budget is answered as it is:
      ~([1 2] | elide 100 | eq [1 2])
  ~~|
  #[::elision]
